::PC_U03_L07_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U03_L07_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.12
Question:
Which feature helps choose a sinusoidal model for periodic data?
{
=A. Amplitude, period, midline, and shift#Correct. These are key features for sine and cosine models.
~B. Only denominator restrictions#This reflects a common misconception; check the unit circle or graph feature.
~C. Only polynomial zeros#This is not supported by the model or context.
~D. Only logarithm base#This belongs to logarithmic functions, not sinusoidal modeling features such as amplitude, period, midline, and shift.
}
::PC_U03_L07_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U03_L07_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.14
Question:
Which statement correctly interprets a trigonometric model?
{
~A. All x-values always make sense in context.#This choice does not match the requested trigonometric concept.
=B. Context can restrict the meaningful domain.#Correct. Real-world periodic models often have contextual restrictions.
~C. Amplitude is always the period.#This is not supported by the model or context.
~D. Midline is never used in modeling.#The midline is important because it represents the central value of a sinusoidal model.
}
